<figure class="visual">
        <figcaption class="visual-cap"><span class="label">Flow anatomy</span><h3>One tuple, one mapped target</h3><p>The mapping dial is conceptual; it does not claim a disclosed AWS hash formula.</p></figcaption>
        <div class="nlb-flow">
          <div class="stage-row"><article class="stage blue"><h4>1 · Client and network access</h4><p>DNS returns zonal NLB addresses. An internet-facing IPv4 NLB can associate one Elastic IP per enabled Availability Zone.</p><code class="stage-code">TCP 203.0.113.24:51514 → 198.51.100.10:443</code></article><article class="stage purple"><h4>2 · Listener and action</h4><p>The listener accepts a configured transport protocol and port. Its default or matching IPv4/IPv6 source-type rule forwards to a target group.</p></article><article class="stage green"><h4>3 · Target health</h4><p>Distributed health checks establish the eligible target set. Zonal health and cross-zone settings can change which nodes and targets serve new flows.</p></article></div>
          <div class="tuple"><span>TCP</span><span>src 203.0.113.24</span><span>sport 51514</span><span>dst 198.51.100.10</span><span>dport 443</span></div>
          <div class="flow-lanes">
            <div class="flow-lane"><div class="flow-label"><strong>Flow A · packets 1, 2, 3</strong><br/>source port 51514</div><div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="nlb-core"><b>NLB</b><small>zonal static address<br/>flow mapping</small></div><div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="flow-label healthy"><strong>10.0.21.18:8443</strong><br/>same target for this flow</div></div>
            <div class="flow-lane"><div class="flow-label"><strong>Flow B · packet 1</strong><br/>source port 51515</div><div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="nlb-core"><b>NLB</b><small>new tuple<br/>new mapping</small></div><div class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="flow-label healthy"><strong>10.0.22.41:8443</strong><br/>may choose another target</div></div>
          </div>
          <div class="tls-modes"><article class="tls-mode"><strong>TCP listener :443</strong><code>encrypted bytes → target</code><p>The target can terminate TLS. NLB does not need to decrypt the HTTP request.</p></article><article class="tls-mode"><strong>TLS listener :443</strong><code>TLS ends at NLB → backend connection</code><p>NLB uses the configured target-group protocol for the target-side connection.</p></article></div>
          <div class="qualified"><strong>Client address is conditional.</strong> Instance targets and several protocol combinations preserve it by default. TCP/TLS IP targets have different defaults and use <code>preserve_client_ip.enabled</code>; topology restrictions also apply.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="service-details"><article><h4>Listener and target protocols</h4><p>Listeners support TCP, TLS, UDP, TCP_UDP, QUIC, and TCP_QUIC. Target-group combinations vary by listener protocol; only a TCP listener can forward to an ALB target.</p></article><article><h4>Flow and connection controls</h4><p>Flow mapping uses transport fields. Configure cross-zone load balancing, deregistration delay, source-IP stickiness where supported, Proxy Protocol v2, and client-IP preservation with care.</p></article><article><h4>Network and operations</h4><p>Security groups can filter NLB traffic when attached. Observe healthy targets, zonal health, active and new flows, reset counts, processed bytes, port allocation errors, and TLS negotiation errors.</p></article></div>
        <div class="behavior-note"><strong>Behavior to keep in mind.</strong> If all registered targets are unhealthy, NLB can fail open to them. DNS may remove an unhealthy zone before its cached address expires. Existing transport connections are not automatically recreated on another target.</div>
        <div class="takeaway"><strong>Static is zonal:</strong> each enabled Availability Zone gets an NLB address; an internet-facing IPv4 NLB can use one Elastic IP per zone.</div>
      </figure>
